// Part 1: Group Discussion — Answers

//1. Looking back at everything you've learned this course, which concept do you think connects to the most other concepts? Why?

//ANSWER//
//Functions connect to many concepts because they can contain calculations, conditions, loops, variables and other operations. They also make programs easier to organize and reuse.


//2. If you were explaining this entire course to a friend who has never coded, in two or three sentences, what would you say JavaScript actually lets you do?

//ANSWER//
// JavaScript allows us to make web pages interactive and perform calculations and operations based on user input. It can also respond to events and manipulate webpage content.


// 3. When building a larger program, why might it help to break it into small functions instead of writing one giant block of code?

//ANSWER//
// Small functions make the program easier to understand, test, debug and reuse. They also prevent us from repeating the same code.


// 4. Think about a bug you fixed earlier in this course. What did that experience teach you about how to approach debugging in general?

//ANSWER//
// Debugging teaches us to identify the error, understand why it happened, test possible fixes and check the program again instead of guessing.

// 5. As a group, discuss: which combination of concepts (e.g., loops + functions, or conditionals + ternaries) do you feel most confident about, and which still feels shak?

//ANSWER//
// As a group, we feel most confident with condition and ternary operators because they are straightforward for making decisions in a program. we still feel a 
// little shaky with loops and functions, especially when combining the together, so we need more practice with them.




